// test-error
// #1322 review: ADR-036's bounds check must use the const of the scope the
// array is declared in, not whichever scope declared that name last.
//
// The program artifact keyed every const by BARE name, so two scopes each
// declaring `N` shared one slot. `this.t[5]` on a `u8[N] t` inside `Small` was
// then bounded by `Big.N` when `Big` happened to be derived later -- and by
// `Small.N` when it did not. Swapping the two scope declarations turned E0854
// on and off, which is the order-dependent diagnostic these analyzers' own
// comments cite #1399 for, arriving through a different map.
//
// The PAIR is the assertion. This file declares `Small` first and its sibling
// declares `Big` first; a lookup that still depends on order passes one and
// fails the other, whichever way it leans. A single file could pass by luck.
//
// `test-error` rather than an execution fixture on purpose: the emitted array
// SIZE is still taken from the collided key (#1538, which folds the dimension
// at declare time, before the program artifact exists), so compiling code here
// would assert C that is known wrong. An error fixture generates none.

scope Small {
    private const u8 N <- 2;
    private u8[N] t;

    public u8 read() {
        return this.t[5];
    }
}

scope Big {
    private const u8 N <- 10;
    private u8 unused <- 0;
}

u8 main() {
    return Small.read();
}
